# Host‐Guest Chemistry of Chiral MOFs for Multicolor Circularly Polarized Luminescence Including Room Temperature Phosphorescence

## abstract
Exploiting the chirality transfer and amplification in the hierarchical chiral systems by the visible and accurate structures is still a challenge. Herein, a pair of homochiral metal‐organic frameworks (MOFs) DCF‐12 and LCF‐12 with high rigidity and high porosity are synthesized via reticular chemistry. Interestingly, these two enantiomers can act as nano‐containers, in which four chromophores, covering acridine, pyrene, 9,10‐Bis(phenylvinyl) anthracene (BPEA), and coronene can be introduced by in situ encapsulation. Importantly, the precise single crystal structures of all guest‐loaded MOFs by X‐ray diffraction technique can be obtained smoothly. It not only clearly reveals the chirality transfer from chiral host framework to achiral guest emitters through space chirality transfer, but also circularly polarized luminescence can be achieved and modulated through the synergistic effect. Extraordinarily, both pyrene@DCF‐12 and pyrene@LCF‐12 exhibit fascinating multi‐color tunable room temperature phosphorescence (RTP) and dynamic circularly polarized luminescence. Besides, the RTP quantum yields of pyrene@DCF‐12 and pyrene@LCD‐12 are high up to 75.39% and 73.43%, which exceeds most of that of RTP materials. These results demonstrate that chiral MOFs can serve as an accurate platform to investigate the mechanism of chirality transfer and amplification and to prompt the development of CPL‐active materials.

Exploiting the chirality transfer and amplification in the hierarchical chiral systems by the visible and accurate structures is still a challenge. Herein, a pair of homochiral metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) DCF-12 and LCF-12 with high rigidity and high porosity are synthesized via reticular chemistry. Interestingly, these two enantiomers can act as nano-containers, in which four chromophores, covering acridine, pyrene, 9,10-Bis(phenylvinyl) anthracene (BPEA), and coronene can be introduced by in situ encapsulation. Importantly, the precise single crystal structures of all guest-loaded MOFs by X-ray diffraction technique can be obtained smoothly. It not only clearly reveals the chirality transfer from chiral host framework to achiral guest emitters through space chirality transfer, but also circularly polarized luminescence can be achieved and modulated through the synergistic effect. Extraordinarily, both pyrene@DCF-12 and pyrene $@$ LCF-12 exhibit fascinating multi-color tunable room temperature phosphorescence (RTP) and dynamic circularly polarized luminescence. Besides, the RTP quantum yields of pyrene@DCF-12 and pyrene@LCD-12 are high up to $75.39\%$ and $73.43\%$ , which exceeds most of that of RTP materials. These results demonstrate that chiral MOFs can serve as an accurate platform to investigate the mechanism of chirality transfer and amplification and to prompt the development of CPL-active materials.
## 1. Introduction
Chirality is crucial to living systems, pharmaceutical industry, and material science.[1] Chiral materials with circularly polarized luminescence (CPL) consisting of the differential luminescence of the left and right circularly polarized light in luminescent systems2 have attracted increasing attention in information storage,[3] biosensing,[4] photoelectric devices.[5] Exploring the origin and evolution of chirality is still a very interesting topic, and equally important for the development of high-performing CPL materials.
To date, tremendous efforts have been made to obtain CPL-active materials. Generally, the strategies are devoted widely to the design and synthesis of chiral metal complexes,[6] chiral organic small molecules[7], and chiral supramolecular systems.[8] Among these methods, the construction of multi-component supramolecular systems, specifically, the host-guest hierarchical systems, associating the incorporation of the guest luminophores into chiral host, has become a popular pathway to achieve the generation, delivery, amplification, and reversion of chirality, including CPL activity from chiral hosts to achiral guests.[9] Moreover, the superiority of this method is that the luminescent guests are diverse and controllable in the species, emission wavelength, and photoresponse.[10] Except for amazing the intriguing strategy and the huge achievement, however, there also exists drawbacks: as soft systems, it is difficult to maintain stability of self-assembly supramolecular systems for a long time,[11] and the dense characteristic also limits the application.[12] Importantly, the multi-component supramolecular systems based on non-covalent interactions belong to amorphous materials, it is difficult to characterize chirality through direct evidence and accurate structure at molecule scale. Besides, circularly polarized room temperature phosphorescence (RTP) based on hostguest systems is still lacking.[13] It is essential to develop novel materials with high porosity, strong rigidity, and good stability to expand the family of CPL materials, and to provide the direct host-guest structures down to molecule level to reveal the evolution of chirality.
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) were assembled through metal ions or clusters and organic struts.[14] As a kind of porous crystalline material, MOFs have inherent advantages for photonic sciences. The ordered arrangement of the $\pi$ -conjugated ligand could significantly enhance the rigid and luminescence quantum yield.[15] Importantly, MOFs could act as multifunctional light-harvesting and transfer platforms and nano-scaled containers to encapsulate a variety of photonic guests, such as organic dyes and fluorescent quantum dots.[16] The porous MOFs, like the solid-state solution, exhibit a confinement effect and effectively suppress or weaken the aggregation-caused quenching (ACQ) of the luminophores.[17] Particularly, compared with the self-assembled supramolecular systems, the more ordered arrangement into MOFs could be achieved for the guest species.[18] Meanwhile, homochiral MOFs play an important role in the field of chirality, which could be synthesized directly by using the enantiomeric ligands. Then, these homochiral MOFs could be utilized to serve as chiral matrices, resulting in the construction of a series of host-guest systems via the incorporation of guests into chiral space.[19] Most important of all, based on the crystalline characteristics of MOFs, the visible and precise structures of guests $\ @\mathrm{MOFs}$ can be obtained through a single crystal X-ray diffraction approach. Totally, these advantages endow MOFs as powerfully chiral platforms to encapsulate guest chromophores for the development of CPL-active materials. Some chiral MOFs have been employed as host matrices to encapsulate guest emitters for the generation of CPL.[20] However, the visible host-guest structures can not be obtained by single-crystal X-ray diffraction. The actual positions and aggregations of the guest emitters into host frameworks are unpredictable. Besides, the CPL property of chiral MOF itself or guests-incorporated MOFs is static without dynamic responsiveness. As a result, it is essential to develop new chiral MOFs for the generation and application of dynamic CPL.
In this contribution, we present a straightforward route to construct highly porous luminescent homochiral MOFs by combining the chiral organic linkers and the luminescent ligand. Enantiomers camphoric acid and 1,1,2,2-tetra(pyridin4-yl)ethene) (tpe) are selected as chiral sources and luminogen to synthesize luminescent MOFs. Two homochiral compounds $[\mathrm{Cd}_{2}(\mathrm{D-cam})_{2}(\mathrm{tpe})]$ (DCF-12) and $[\mathrm{Cd}_{2}(\mathrm{L}\mathrm{-cam})_{2}(\mathrm{tpe})]$ (LCF-12) $\langle\mathrm{DCF}=\textbf{D}$ -configuration chiral ligand-based chiral framework, $\mathrm{\DeltaD/L{\cdot}C a m\ =\Delta D/L}$ -camphoric acid) featuring high porosity and good stability were prepared. Through utilizing the two compounds as host scaffolds, a series of host-guest compositions were fabricated through the one-pot encapsulation of guest emitters. Interestingly, the single crystal structures of guests $@$ MOFs can be directly obtained by single crystal X-ray diffrac­ tion analysis, revealing that chirality transfer could be successfully achieved from the chiral space of frameworks to the achiral guests. Significantly, the chiral excited state was also transferred from host to guest emitters, as a result, the host-guest materials exhibit the highly efficient CPL property. It is worth noting that pyrene $@$ DCF-12 and pyrene $@$ LCD-12 have excitation wavelength-dependent room temperature phosphorescence (RTP) and dynamic circularly polarized luminescence. Besides, the phosphorescence quantum yield of pyrene $@$ DCF-12 is up to $75.39\%$ . Furthermore, the mechanism of chirality delivery and energy transfer were also investigated. These results indicate that encapsulation engineering of chiral MOFs provides a promising way to develop chiroptical materials.
## 2. Results and Discussion
## 2.1. Host Matrices
Single crystals of compounds DCF-12 and LCF-12 with high purity were synthesized by the solvothermal reaction of $\mathbf{D}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{cam}$ , tpe, and $\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in DMA/Benzyl alcohol/ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . Single crystal X-ray diffraction analysis reveals that both DCF-12 and LCF-12 crystallize in an orthorhombic system with chiral space group I222. The structure of DCF-12 was described in
detail. The asymmetric unit contains one independent ${\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ ion, one d-cam, and half of tpe molecule. Two ${\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ ions are connected by four d-cam to generate the paddle-wheel secon­ dary building block $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{COO})_{4}]$ , forming a wave-like 2D layer $[Z\mathrm{n}_{2}(\mathrm{p}\mathrm{-cam})_{4}]$ . Tpe molecules coordinated $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ions also to yield 2D sheets. These two kinds of layers were further crosslinked via $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{COO})_{4}]$ nodes. The overall network is a noninterpenetrated framework containing three-dimensional channels. The largest channel is in the direction of $c$ -axis, and the window cross-section of the hexagonal channels is $7.2\times12.4\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{2}$ (Figure S1a, Supporting Information). The channels also present hexahedral along $a$ -axis with the size of ${\approx}9.2\times9.7\times12.3\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{3}$ (Figure 1b). The free space in compound DCF-12 is $45.0\%(4230.9\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{3}$ of $9406.7\mathrm{~\AA~}^{3}$ unit cell volume) after removal of solvent molecules by PLATON.[21] Furthermore, as shown in Figure 1b and Figure S2 (Supporting Information), although tpe molecules display the parallel arrangement, no obvious $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking interactions can be observed due to the large spacing $({\approx}9.2\mathrm{-}\mathrm{}\mathrm{}\bar{10}.1\mathrm{~}\mathring{\mathrm{A}})$ . Importantly, tpe molecules exhibit the same conformation, showing the left-handed helical shape, M enantiomorph. The average dihedral angles between double bond plane and pyridine rings of tpe in DCF-12 are $55.67^{\circ}$ , $55.71^{\circ}~55.67^{\circ}$ and $57.71^{\circ}$ , respectively (Figure  1c), and the orderly arrangement of tpe molecules endows the formation of helical chains (Figure  1e). Correspondingly, tpe molecules display P-type conformation in LCF-12 (Figure  1d). It indicates that the layer consisting of tpe and d-cam or l-cam is the chiral structure, it can be concluded that the chiral layer $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{p}\mathrm{-cam})_{4}]$ and nodes induced synergistically the chirality generation of tpe molecules. Similarly, the chirality of tetraphenylethylene results from the steric hindrance of the substituted phenyl rings,[22] and the metal nodes are just like substituent sections.
## 2.2. Optical Properties of Host Compounds
In order to check the chirality property of DCF-12 and LCF-12, the solid-state circular dichroism (CD) spectra of power samples of each compound were measured, and the results exhibited the mirror-imaged CD signals. The negative Cotton effect was observed at ca. 247 and $325\ \mathrm{nm}$ for DCF-12, which was ascribed to d-cam and the formation of a left-handed tpe moiety (M-type). Accordingly, positive Cotton effect at ${\approx}246$ and $325\mathrm{nm}$ for LCF-12 (Figure 3e), which is consistent with P-type configuration of tpe in CLF-12 and UV–vis spectra of LCF-12 (Figure 3d and S27). Meanwhile, photoluminescence and circularly polarized luminescence were investigated under ambient conditions. DCF-12 and LCF-12 show weak green fluorescence with emission centers located at 540 and $541\mathrm{nm}$ following excitation at $365\ \mathrm{nm}$ , respectively (Figure  3a). The lifetimes of DCF-12 and LCF-12 are 10.01 and $10.44~\mathrm{ns}$ , along with the fluorescence quantum yields of $1.47\%$ and $1.53\%$ . In addition, the CPL information can not be detected, largely due to the weak luminescent ability.
With the introduction of acridine, two host-guest compounds of $\mathrm{Ar}@\mathrm{DCF}\cdot12$ and $\mathrm{Ar@LCF}\ –12$ were prepared. The color of two crystals could change from transparent to faint yellow under the irradiation of $365\ \mathrm{nm}$ UV lamp (Figure  3b). And the crystal states and morphologies of $\mathrm{Ar}@\mathrm{DCF}\cdot12$ and $\mathrm{Ar@LCF}\ –12$ are similar to parents. Besides, the PXRD patterns of $\operatorname{Ar}@\operatorname{DCF-}12$ and $\mathrm{Ar}@\mathrm{LCF}\cdot12$ also matched well with the original simulated ones of relative parents (Figure S9c, Supporting Information), reflecting good structural stability of $\mathrm{Ar}@\mathrm{DCF}\cdot12$ and $\mathrm{Ar}@\mathrm{LCF}\cdot12$ . Likewise, the single crystals of the other three guests-embedded MOFs were also obtained, and these crystals show guest-dependent color change (Figure 3b,c; Figure S24, Supporting Information). Importantly, all the above guests-incorporated MOFs show high crystallinity. Therefore, we could visualize clearly the host-guest structures obtained by single crystal X-ray diffraction analysis. Besides, the inter­ molecular spacing of the parallel aligned acridine molecules is up to 19.31 Å (Figure S4, Supporting Information), indicating that the acridine molecules formed discrete monomer rather than excimer aggregation.[23] By comparison, pyrene $@$ DCF-12 and pyrene $@$ LCF-12, BPEA $@$ DCF-12, and BPEA $@$ LCF-12 show similar structural feature and modes of guest arrangement. And BPEA molecules are still anchored in the interspace of tpe. Meanwhile, the large hexahedral cages stretching in three directions keep empty (Figure 2a–c). However, when the guests were replaced by coronene molecules with the larger cross-section, the crystal structures of coronene $@$ DCF-12 and coronene $@$ LCF-12 possess new achiral space group, Immm, but with the same unit cell dimensions similar to DCF-12 and LCF-12.
Based on the accurate host-guest structures of guests-carried frameworks, the encapsulation engineering and chirality transfer between chiral frameworks and achiral luminophores were further investigated. Firstly, all the guest molecules were packaged into the pores and between tpe molecules, while the adjacent pores with relatively larger free space were empty, and the guest luminophores were encapsulated at regular intervals. According to the above structure analysis of DCF-12/LCF-12, two kinds of pores in the host framework, in the processes of in situ encapsulation, all guest molecules were introduced into the smaller pores, acridine, pyrene, and BPEA can well accommodate in the smaller pore, not causing the disorder of host frameworks. Compared to acridine, pyrene and BPEA, the incorporation of the coronene molecule can lead to the disorder of D/L-cam and tpe, but the coronene molecules were still encapsulated into the smaller pores rather than the larger pores. If the coronene molecules were introduced into the larger pores, maybe they can not destroy the configuration of organic linkers, the host-guest compounds can maintain the chiral structure. On the whole, the introduction of guest molecules into host framework presents a certain degree of selectivity. We also noticed that there were no hydrogen-bonding inter­ actions associated between guests and frameworks. Despite the face-to-face parallel stacking could be observed clearly, there are only weak $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions between adjacent tpe unit and guest molecules due to the long spatial distance. Totally, the guest species seemed to be suspended in the channels through van der Waals interactions, which could be largely assigned to spatial segmentation and confinement effect of the host frameworks. More than that, the accurate host-guest structures can clarify the evolution of chirality in hierarchical structures. One guest molecule was immobilized between two tpe molecules, resulting in a chiral sandwich structure (Figures  1f and  2e–g), the plane center of guest molecule and the space center of two tpe molecules overlap completely, the guest molecule was fully integrated into the interior of the chiral cavity, the guest molecule becomes a part of chiral space, the chiral cavity can endow the chirality of guest molecule, the chirality was successfully transferred from host framework to guest species through noncovalent bonding interactions and space chirality transfer from chiral host framework to achiral guest emitters.[24] Therefore, chiral space plays a very important role in chirality transmission and amplification. In this regard, homochiral and porous MOFs can be regarded as solid-state solvents, exhibiting the intrinsic advantages to act as host matrices, and providing the visible structures to trace the evolution of chirality. However, other supramolecular systems based on non-covalent inter­actions, such as liquid crystals, multicomponent supra­molecular gels cannot be achieved because of amorphous structures.
## 2.3. Optical Properties of Guest-Encapsulated Systems
Subsequently, the photophysical properties of host-guest compounds were further investigated. Ar@DCF-12 and $\mathrm{Ar}@\mathrm{LCF}\cdot12$ showed gray-blue under UV light and a blue emission peak centered at ${\approx}454$ nm was detected by exciting at $365~\mathrm{nm}$ . The quantum yields are $0.75\%$ and $0.70\%$ , respectively. Impressively, BPEA $@$ DCF-12 and BPEA $@$ LCF-12 crystals are bright yellow and exhibit intense green emission (Figure 3c), showing high quantum yields of $27.30\%$ and $24.12\%$ (Figures S41 and S42, Supporting Information), along with lifetimes of 12.31 and 12.43 ns, respectively. Particularly, the multiple emission for coronene $@$ DCF-12 and coronene $@$ LCF-12 was observed in the range from 425 to $525~\mathrm{nm}$ . Collectively, the host-guest systems exhibit guest luminophore-dominated emission behaviors, the guests play a major role in controlling the emission wavelength and luminescent efficiency. Noticeably, the emission peaks of the host-guest systems are considerably close to the fluorescence emission of the corresponding dopants, which were obtained in their dilute solution, particularly for coroneneinserted systems (Figure S20, Supporting Information).[25] These results demonstrated that the encapsulation of guest emitters into MOFs could significantly diminish aggregationcaused quenching, and the guests were confined into channels featuring the state of monomer dispersion, not forming the exciplex or excimer,[26] which was also in agreement with the results of the single crystal structure of host-guest compounds. Taken together, on the basis of the host-guest structures and photophysical properties of the guest-encapsulated MOFs, the mechanism of the chirality transfer and amplification from chiral framework to chiral guest emitters, the CPL properties in chiral host-guest MOFs (well-ground powder) could be illustrated.
Both strong CD signals were observed for Ar@DCF-12 and Ar@LCF-12 (Figure  3f; Figure S23, Supporting Information), BPEA $@$ DCF-12, and BPEA $@$ LCF-12 (Figure  3g). The absorption bands show an obvious red shift compared to that of DCF-12 and LCF-12, the absorption wavelengths of BPEA $@$ DCF-12 and BPEA $@$ LCF-12 over $400\mathrm{nm}$ originated from BPEA (Figure  3d; Figures S22 and S27, Supporting Information). In other words, apart from the contribution of chiral camphoric acid, the helical backbones, which were formed through the coordination assembly induced-stacking of tpe molecules, play an important role in enhancing CD generation. To our delight, BPEA $@$ DCF-12 and BPEA $@$ LCF-12 displayed intense CPL, the positive and negative CPL signals could be seen at 533 and 534 nm under the irradiation of UV light at $340\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Figure  3h), indicating that the strong chiral induction ability of chiral channels, the hierarchical chiral cages consisting of chiral camphoric acid and helical tpe molecules can efficiently transfer chirality to the achiral guest emitters. In contrast, CPL intensities of $\mathrm{Ar}@\mathrm{DCF}\cdot12$ and $\mathrm{Ar}@\mathrm{LCF}\cdot12$ were below the detection limit, due to the strong absorption and the weak emission. As for coronene@DCF-12 and coronene $@$ LCF-12, no solid-state CPL signals were detected, proving yet again that the host framework is achiral, and can not endow achiral guests with chirality.
Interestingly, after the introduction of pyrene molecules, the bright and transparent crystals pyrene $@$ DCF-12 and pyrene $@$ LCF-12 were constructed. Pyrene $@$ DCF-12 shows the green emission with the central peak at $518~\mathrm{nm}$ (excited at $365~\mathrm{nm}$ ) (Figure S34a, Supporting Information). Surprisingly, it can observe the naked-eye shiny red afterglow lasting ${\approx}2.5$ s after turning off $365\mathrm{nm}\mathrm{UV}$ irradiation (Movie S1, Supporting Information). The delayed luminescence was detected. Extraordinary, we found that it exhibited excitation-dependent afterglow emission behaviors, the steady luminescence spectra clearly displayed that the afterglow color could change from yellow to orange to red under the varied excitation wavelength from 400 to $620~\mathrm{nm}$ , accordingly, the range of emission peaks was red-shifted from 550 to $670\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 4a).
In order to verify the afterglow property, the luminescent emission was performed under different temperatures, and the emission intensity gradually decreased with the increase of temperature from 123 to 298 K (Figure S30, Supporting Information), indicating the delayed afterglow was assigned to a long-lived phosphorescence, excluding the possibility that delayed afterglow is the thermally-activated delayed fluorescence (TADF).[27] The excitation-photoluminescence mapping of pyrene@DCF-12 indicates that excitation-dependent phosphorescence results from multiple emission centers (Figure  4b). The CIE coordinates of the photoluminescent peaks depicted the excitation-responsive multi-color tunable phosphorescence emission (Figure 4c). Meanwhile, time-resolved emission-decay spectra showed that the RTP lifetime of pyrene $@$ DCF-12 is up to 0.34 s under ambient conditions. More importantly, the phosphorescence quantum yield could reach $75.39\%$ , over most of the phosphorescence-active materials including but not limited to organic phosphorescence materials.[28] Besides, we found that the naked-eye room temperature phosphorescence of pyrene $@$ DCF-12 vanished after being heated and dried. Interestingly, RTP can be recovered immediately, when pyrene $@$ DCF-12 crystals were soaked or eluted by benzyl alcohol (BtOH). The reversible fluorescence-phosphorescence transformation could be recycled many times.
Subsequently, we analyzed the phosphorescence behaviors of the solution and solid of the pyrene molecules at $77~\mathrm{K}$ . The pyrene molecules showed multiple weak peaks in the solution or at solid state, the wavelengths of the intense peaks are all over $600\ \mathrm{nm}$ , and the main emission peaks in solid state are distributed at 650 and $702~\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure S31, Supporting Information). Just as previous report, it has been demonstrated that the pyrene molecules have deep-red phosphorescence at low temperatures.[29] Importantly, the spectral trends were in almost total agreement with the phosphorescence emission of pyrene $@$ DCF-12. Meanwhile, given that no afterglow signal for tpe molecules could be detected, it could largely confirm that the phosphorescence of pyrene $@$ DCF-12 originated from the guest pyrene molecules. Besides, the phosphorescence characteristics of the mechanically-mixed host-guest systems with the corresponding molar ratio were checked, only fluorescence but no phosphorescence performance was detected, even if the processes were assisted by benzyl alcohol (Figure S32, Supporting Information).
In order to evaluate the solvent response of RTP of pyrene $@$ DCF-12, other nine kinds of organic solvents were selected as triggers. As shown in Figure S33 (Supporting Information), the dried pyrene $@$ DCF-12 crystals were soaked into the above solvents for 2 hours, the solvent-activated samples have some differences under natural light and $365\ \mathrm{nm}$ UV lamp. Unexpectedly, all solvent-active samples show the naked-eye red afterglow after removing the UV excitation sources. The reversible cyclicity was also investigated. The solvent-soaked samples were dried at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ again, and the color of the samples return yellow, then the samples were immersed into the ten solvents, and it can be found that the solvent-activated samples also show the corresponding fluorescence and red afterglow under UV irradiation and after turning off the excita­tion (Figure S33a–e, Supporting Information). A similar pheno­menon was also could be observed after eight successive recycles. Meanwhile, the stability of solvent-activated samples was investigated, and the high stability can be confirmed by the powder X-ray diffraction (Figure S33f,g, Supporting Information). Importantly, benefiting from the high crystallinity of pyrene $@$ DCF-12, we can obtain the accurate and visible host-guest structure of pyrene@DCF-12 by single-crystal X-ray diffraction. Compared with the host-guest structures of pyrene $@$ DCF-12 before and after benzyl alcohol treatment, the host-guest structures of pyrene $@$ DCF-12 have no changes, indicating that the arrangement of pyrene molecules and the configuration of host framework can keep well, also suggesting that the energy transfer between pyrene molecules and host framework is hardly affected by benzyl alcohol. Totally, the main role of solvent molecules may increase the passage of the intersystem crossing, further facilitating the generation of the room temperature phosphorescence (Figure 4f).[30]
To deeply understand the origin of ultralong room temperature phosphorescence, the dynamic photophysical parameters $k_{\mathrm{r}},k_{\mathrm{nr}},$ and $k_{\mathrm{isc}}$ were evaluated based on the following equations, where $k_{\mathrm{r}},k_{\mathrm{nr}},$ and $k_{\mathrm{isc}}$ are the radiative and nonradiative decay rates of phosphorescence and intersystem crossing rate from singlet to triplet excited states. We can obtain that $k_{\mathrm{isc}}$ reaches up to $8.94\times10^{7}~\mathrm{s}^{\boxed{1}}$ , benefiting the generation of triplet excited states, such a high value can match many high-performing phosphorescence materials.[31] The $k_{\mathrm{r}}$ $_{\textrm{r}}(2.22\ \mathrm{s^{-1}})$ far exceeds the $k_{\mathrm{nr}}$ $(0.72~\mathrm{s}^{-1})$ , indicating that nonradiative transitions can be intensively suppressed through the confinement of the rigid host-guest framework, which is favorable to the generation of long-lived phosphorescence.[32]
Furthermore, the singlet and triplet energy levels of the guest emitters and tpe ligand were calculated, the quantitative models were employed from the extraction of the crystal structures. The energy gap $\Delta E_{\mathrm{ST}}$ of the $S_{1}$ and $T_{1}$ states of pyrene molecule is $1.92\ \mathrm{eV},$ such a large $\Delta E_{\mathrm{ST}}$ makes it strong against intersystem crossing. Nevertheless, the energy gaps between the $S_{1}$ state of pyrene and the $T_{1}$ state of tpe molecule are down to 0.34 eV, which is favorable to excitonic ISC (Figure  4d,e). In addition, the maximum excitation wavelength of pyrene appears to red shift in host-guest compound than itself, indicating that the excitons of pyrene molecules could be promoted with the aid of triplet state-triplet state energy transfer between host and guest emitters, further facilitating the generation of phosphorescence. A similar mechanism has been demonstrated in some hostguest-doped systems.[33] These results turned out that the synergistic effect of the introduction of the guests into MOFs is of great significance to the generation of phosphorescence activity. Significantly, CD and CPL spectrum of the dried pyrene $@$ DCF-12 and pyrene $@$ LCF-12 showed an obvious red shift. Especially, when excited at $365\mathrm{nm}$ , the CPL band covers a large scope ranging from 475 to $650~\mathrm{nm}$ , exhibiting dual emission. And the maximum peak at $525\ \mathrm{nm}$ belongs to fluorescence. Meanwhile, the longer emission at $612~\mathrm{\nm}$ arises from phosphorescence (Figure  4i). Just like DCF-12 and LCF-12, a negative Cotton effect for pyrene $@$ DCF-12 and a strong positive Cotton effect for pyrene $@$ LCF-12 were observed, and CPL signals of pyrene $@$ DCF-12 and pyrene $\ @\mathrm{LCF}.12$ are mirror symmetrical. The values of $\mathrm{\g_{lum}}$ of pyrene $@$ DCF-12 and pyrene $@$ LCF-12 were $\approx3.7\times10^{-3}$ and $5.1\times10^{-3}$ , where $\mathrm{g}_{\mathrm{lum}}$ is defined as $2(I_{\mathrm{L}}-I_{\mathrm{R}})/(I_{\mathrm{L}}+I_{\mathrm{R}})$ ; where $I_{\mathrm{L}}$ and $I_{\mathrm{R}}$ refer to the intensity of the left- and right-handed CPL, respectively,[34] which was moderate among the reported organic self-assembled materials (Figure  4j).[35] More importantly, upon being triggered by benzyl alcohol, where $30~\mathrm{mg}$ crystal samples were wetted by adding drops of $0.05~\mathrm{mL}$ benzyl alcohol, reversible on–off circularly polarized phosphorescence signal transformations were tracked. The region of luminescent emission got wide and the phosphorescence emission could red-shift to $650~\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure S34c,d, Supporting Information), demonstrating that not only ground-state chirality but also excited-state chirality including triplet excited state could be transferred and amplified through the host-guest self-assembly. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first time that stimuli-responsive CPL with RTP property was reported in the host-guest coordination polymer systems.[36]
Finally, taking advantage of the multiple emission including fluorescence and phosphorescence, the potential anti-counterfeiting application was exploited. As shown in Figure 4g, A tree trunk pattern was fabricated, where the top half of the tree was made of pyrene $@$ DCF-12 powder, as well as BPEA $@$ DCF-12 for the bottom half. This pattern shows yellow and green colors under 365 nm UV irradiation, then UV irradiation was turned off, accompanied by the disappearance of green emission, and the red afterglow can be seen. Furthermore, once the pattern was heated, BtOH solvent molecules could be removed, and no naked-eye delayed emission could be observed. Interestingly, the red afterglow can be displayed again through the adsorption of BtOH. Thus, the solvent-responsive phosphorescence materials can realize the multilevel anti-counterfeitings.
## 3. Conclusion
In summary, a pair of homochiral porous coordination polymers DCF-12 and LCF-12 were synthesized through coordinationoriented self-assembly. The stable frameworks can act as rigid matrices, and four kinds of guest chromophores were successfully encapsulated into the free space of the host frameworks. Benefiting from the periodic lattice of host framework, it can accurately obtain the single crystal structures of guest-loaded compounds, in which the visible and well-defined arrangement of guest molecules could be observed, it clearly revealed that the encapsulation of guest species into voids mainly depended on the inherent selectivity of the pores rather than size match effect. Through the high-order introduction of guest chromophores, not only the chirality of host matrix could be transferred to achiral guests, but also the luminescent efficiency could be greatly enhanced by guest chromophores. Simultaneously, based on the synergistic effect of host-guest systems, intense CD and CPL signals could be generated. Besides, the excitation wavelength-dependent ultralong RTP and stimulusresponsive CPL was achieved in pyrene-encapsulated host-guest system. Importantly, the mechanism of chirality transfer from the chiral host to the achiral guest emitters could be completely understood via the accurate host-guest structures. This work represents one of the very few examples of multi-component supramolecular self-assembly for CPL-active materials with fluorescent-phosphorescence transformation, also indicates that MOFs can serve as an important chirality platform to encapsulate guest chromophores for the chirality transfer and amplification, providing the accurate models for the exploration of chirality mechanism.
